<h1>Orcraft for AI agents</h1>
<p>An Orcraft suite is ordinary Python, and its configuration is declared as class attributes. An AI
  agent such as Claude Code can therefore work with a suite without an API to learn and without the
  controller GUI. To run a test, the AI agent writes a small Python file that fills in the values it
  needs, runs it and reads the log. To extend a suite, it writes new components the way the existing
  ones are written. Everything the AI agent writes is an ordinary suite: the person opens it in the
  controller GUI later, reviews it, runs it and corrects what needs correcting. This section is
  written for the AI agent as much as for the person instructing it.</p>

<h2 id="requests">Example requests</h2>
<p>A request names what to do and where to do it. The examples below are only examples: any request
  about the suites, the machines and the logs works the same way.</p>

<h3 id="getting-started-requests">Getting started requests</h3>
<p>A getting started request creates a suite where none exists. Its source is the documentation of
  the system under test: a README, a user manual, an API specification, a requirements document or
  the source code itself. The request names that source, and the AI agent reads it to decide what the
  groups are and what each first test checks. It then creates the suite repository with the suite
  file, one folder per group and a first test in each, laid out like the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/">tutorial suite</a>. The new suite is then
  <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/getting-started/#add-the-suite">added to the controller</a>, and
  every later request grows it.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("Create a suite for the maze application from its README: one group with one test that starts it and checks it exits cleanly.") }}
{{ cmd.copyPath("Read the maze application's user manual and write a suite with a group per feature, like the tutorial.") }}
