<h3 id="run-requests">Run requests</h3>
<p>A run request names an existing test, group or suite and the machines to run it on. The AI agent
  writes a configured copy of the item, runs it and reports from the log.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("Run the TCP Traffic group: Linux machines as fan-out, Windows machines as link partners.") }}
{{ cmd.copyPath("Run the network suite with the macOS machine as SUT and lab-win-1 as link partner.") }}

<h3 id="development-requests">Development requests</h3>
<p>A development request adds to a suite. The AI agent writes the new component in the suite
  repository, following the structure of the existing ones, with the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/">Framework reference</a> as its guide. The edited suite is
  then <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/getting-started/#add-the-suite">added to the controller</a>
  like any other suite and run by hand for polishing.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("Add a bidirectional traffic test to the TCP Traffic group; fail below 900 Mbit/s.") }}
{{ cmd.copyPath("Add a Solve Timeout parameter to the maze suite's Solve tests, default 5 seconds.") }}

<h3 id="continuous-expansion">Setting the AI agent to expand coverage</h3>
<p>Coverage does not have to wait for someone to notice a gap. A standing instruction, run on a
  schedule or from the pipeline of the system under test, sets the AI agent to compare what the
  system does with what the suite checks and to add what is missing: a case in a matrix, a test in a
  group, a group for a feature the suite does not touch yet. Each pass ends with the new tests run
  once and a commit for the person to review.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("Every night, compare the maze application's features with the maze suite and add tests for what is not covered.") }}
{{ cmd.copyPath("On every merge to the router firmware, read the changed code and add the network suite tests that cover it.") }}
{{ cmd.copyPath("Every day, review the TCP Traffic matrix against the NIC's supported MTUs and fill in the missing cases.") }}

<h3 id="bug-coverage">Setting the AI agent to cover fixed bugs</h3>
<p>A bug that was fixed once can come back. A standing instruction sets the AI agent to turn every
  fix in the system under test into a test: it reads the bug report or the fixing commit, writes a
  test that fails the way the bug did, and adds it where it belongs in the suite. From then on, the
  suite reports the bug's return on the first run that shows it.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("Every night, for every bug closed today in the maze tracker, add a test to the maze suite that reproduces it.") }}
{{ cmd.copyPath("Bug 4127, the solver looping on a maze without exit, is fixed; add a test that fails on the old build.") }}
{{ cmd.copyPath("Read the router's 2.3.1 release notes and add a network suite test for each fixed bug.") }}

<h3 id="analysis-requests">Analysis requests</h3>
<p>An analysis request is about the logs of earlier runs. The AI agent reads them and answers from
  what they record.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("Compare last night's TCP Transmit logs from the macOS machine and lab-linux-2.") }}
{{ cmd.copyPath("Explain why TCP Receive failed on lab-linux-2 in the last run.") }}

<h3 id="mixed-requests">Mixed requests</h3>
<p>A mixed request does several of these at once. The AI agent writes the component, runs it on the
  machines named and reads the log. When the run shows a defect in the new code, it fixes the code and
  runs again. The person receives the finished test together with its first passing run, and the
  request can end with the commit that lands it.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("Add an IPv6 group like TCP Traffic and run it: Linux as fan-out, Windows as partners.") }}
{{ cmd.copyPath("Add a TCP retransmit test, run it on lab-linux-1, and when it passes commit and push.") }}

<h2 id="machines">Machines and roles</h2>
<p>A machine in a request can be named by host name, by address or by operating system. All of
  these resolve to the entries of the controller's
  <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/endpoints/">endpoints file</a>, the one list of machines
  the AI agent has: each entry carries the host name, the address and the operating system the machine
  reported.</p>
<p>When a test has several endpoint fields, the request also says which machine plays which part.
  In the examples above, "as fan-out" and "as link partners" name the two endpoint fields of the
  traffic tests: the machine under test, with one run per machine, and the set of partners that every
  run shares. The AI agent finds the fields and their types in the test's config class.</p>

